  "unit": "together-against",
  "title": "Prefixes That Change Shape",
  "short": "Many prefixes change their last letter to match the root that follows: in- to im-, con- to col-, ad- to ac-, sub- to sup-.",
  "trouble": "A doubled consonant after a short prefix (acc-, aff-, coll-, imm-, irr-, supp-) is often the sign of a prefix that changed its last letter.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.con-syn",
    "roots.ab-ad-pro",
    "roots.not"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Say “in-possible” quickly, and your lips close for the <i>p</i> before you finish the <i>n</i>. Over centuries, speakers smoothed that bump, and the prefix became <b>im-</b>: <i>impossible</i>. Many prefixes that end in <i>n</i>, <i>d</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>s</i> or <i>x</i> change their last letter to match the next sound. It even has a name, <b>assimilation</b>, from <i>ad-</i> + <i>simil</i> (like): “making alike.”"
      },
      {
